Schedule revision provenance is explicit. The correspondence-format and recipe slots are local stamps of the closed bridge representation and selected registered LC family, each 1; no incoming independently supplied value exists for either, so neither performs a constant-against-itself check. The lowering slot is the local selected Product/root/reciprocal schedule revision 1, also a stamp with no check at join. The exact-arithmetic slot is different: the matrix lowering contract expects 1, and `authenticate_join` reads the actual `Request::arithmetic` from the sealed receipt's authenticated CAS request and compares it with that expected value for one Work. Bindings translation currently writes 1 in `correspondence/translation/request.rs`; that fact is not substituted for reading the actual sealed request. Commit 2 adds this parameter/comparison to the frozen authentication operation, which now costs three Work and still returns only success/refusal. Later consumer comparisons against stored schedule stamps remain separate phase obligations. These local stamps incur no vacuous native revision comparisons; only the sealed arithmetic comparison is charged.

Choose owning closure capture at join. Allocate the existing `LcClosure` and exact-capacity `LcSourceRow` backing once; each row retains its six symbol edges and one existing mapped-source `Rc<PinRecord>` edge. That pin already retains the immutable incoming host/map/mask/companion closure. Sharing this private record creates no new public pin record or pin-table slot; its prepaid release keeps the existing pin witness and ownership live. Reserve at join under (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-reservation`) and (`dec:nrz:matrix-loans-immutable-sharing`): two native allocation debits and their non-cloneable tokens fund the header, exact N-row backing and all seven owning edges per row before allocation or sharing. The backing's single debit includes its complete positional edge inventory; no unfunded pin share exists between join and pending admission. The closure moves into the later checked pending owner with those same two tokens, not a copied row array or newly created debit. A borrowed prepared alternative could safely retain `LcInputReaders` only by adding its heap/tile lifetime to the facade; `LcInputInventory::readers` and `LcShapeTile::current_reader` borrow the heap for that scope. It would prevent the short mutable heap access required for pending allocation until the borrow ended, requiring another consuming capture transition and refusal carrier. Capturing now gives the prepared facade the accepted inventory-only lifetime and a detached immutable native owner, with one explicit capture boundary.

The join reservation uses two closed LC allocation roles (closure header and row backing) in the existing debit inventory, each with a weak expiration witness to the same LcClosure and a heap/account/join owner identity, not a fictitious numerical occupation. The plan fixes both debit slots, capacity, keys and receipt identity before allocation; the guard tracks prepaid allocation cleanup. The `Debit` and `Token` values are built after `guard.finish()`, the last fallible step; the final no-fail commit installs their weak witnesses and moves the tokens with the facade. The receipt holds the two tokens; the heap keeps the corresponding debit records, and neither debit record retains the payload strongly. The closure and receipt reuse the same fresh join identity because they are installed atomically and never replaced independently. Both native tokens use that join identity for `Token.receipt`, their cleanup receipt, because they are installed and released with the join receipt; there is no separately allocated cleanup identity. If implementation requires a distinct cleanup identity, its Work and inventory must be raised explicitly before acceptance, never added silently. Allocate two additional nonwrapping debit keys, one for each allocation; those keys distinguish allocations and are not additional closure identities. On pre-publication failure the guard closes only its acquired prefix; on facade drop it releases the owning edges without new Work, and charged reconciliation observes the expired weak witnesses before removing the conservative debits. Keep the closure header/control block charged while weak records remain. An expired header debit may be released only after no rows debit with the same `LcJoin` owner remains, independent of slot order. The dependency walk charges one Work per debit slot inspected; a paused or Work-exhausted walk retains the header debit. A deferred expired header requests another reconciliation pass, whose slot visits are charged normally. Each actual LC debit release also charges one Work for the existing reconciliation inspection, separately from prepaid destruction. Pending admission authenticates the two tokens and moves them into the existing closure/rows slots of LcDebits, retaining the same owner identity, keys and weak witnesses through the later storage lifetime. It creates only the other seventeen native debit records. The registry variants, cleanup and reconciliation needed to support detached prepared ownership are part of the complete bridge commit; they cannot be deferred until numerical pending implementation. This follows the existing reservation rules without a token-free interval.

Raw tickets cannot replace this ownership. The reason is not identity reuse: (`def:nrz:matrix-loans-identities`) and (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-slot-states`) require nonwrapping generations and forbid a later occupant from satisfying an old ticket; `heap/identity.rs` implements checked successors. Tickets are diagnostic locators, not retaining edges. Under (`dec:nrz:matrix-loans-immutable-sharing`), only owning pins retain immutable payload and dependencies; `loan/reader.rs::current_record` additionally matches the current registry owner and pin witness, and rejects a retired or changed occupation. The prepared receipt therefore owns the closure rather than caching tickets alone. Later checked pending admission still re-authenticates every captured current occupation and companion before allocating output; retained historical bytes do not authorize a new current application. A changed occupation requires fresh preparation/admission even for equal numerical values. Any pre-creation refusal returns the unchanged prepared facade.

The bridge creates no checked pending owner and grants no numerical publication authority. The checked omega slice meets (`dec:nrz:matrix-loans-lc-origin-admission`); `OriginUnavailable` remains the binding-only numerical status. Existing binding-only `LcOutputPending` is never upgraded, including after that gate. Numerical storage, demand/outcome partition, execution, publication, retry and historical output readers belong to the distinct later checked-application route.

**Rule (LC bridge evidence and staged implementation)** · `rule:nrz:matrix-loans-lc-bridge-evidence`

