**Open Question (Two-input representation and original-constructor boundary)** · `open:nrz:matrix-loans-binary-implementation-boundary`

The binary recipe and original correspondence constructor are prerequisites of the matrix operation surface selected here; the scalar primitives alone do not supply them. The chosen slice must implement and independently inventory that original checked constructor as well as the shared two-pin adapter. Its symbolic Work depends on the supplied original region/descriptor construction and must not be represented by the masked admission's fixed figures. The side size 1592 and unchanged shared enum layouts require the independent ABI mirror specified above; the side box is part of this one common mechanism, not a retained native program. If that representation cannot preserve the unary widths, or original correspondence needs a retained authority/program beyond the explicitly inventoried box, stop at this boundary and report the dependency. The smaller next slice is the shared two-input mechanism with Product and the same complete ownership/resource acceptance, followed by Quotient's domain/dispatch controls. A second lifetime mechanism, certificate program, changed unary pins or a broadened input representation is not an acceptable way to force the pair into one implementation commit.

## Published results as unary inputs · `sec:nrz:matrix-loans-chains`

**Decision (Chained masked applications through one unary input)** · `dec:nrz:matrix-loans-chained-applications`

Admit a published binary64 center/total/error result as the single input of Square, SquareRoot or Reciprocal. Square also admits binary32 continuation under (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-mapped-square`), except that binary32 Product and Quotient results refuse chained consumers with `UnsupportedContract`; a chained Square refusal preserves the original pin and makes zero numerical reads or allocation attempts (`[MATRIX-test:unit:loan-binary32-binary-chain-refusal]`). Its producer may be one of those unary members, or an ordered host-origin Product or Quotient under (`dec:nrz:matrix-loans-binary-members`). A unary producer may itself consume an admitted result. Mapped-copy producers also preserve current lineage under (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-mapped-movement`). This admits Product followed by SquareRoot followed by Reciprocal, the represented-input numerical chain needed for angular frequency under (`req:nrz:lc-evaluation-kernel-interface`), without claiming the physical LC binding, guard or graph contract in (`req:nrz:lc-evaluation-matrix-owner`). Choose this slice because a unary consumer already has one exact source capture and one transferred pin; the existing binary producer already seals both original operands. A binary consumer with a chained operand would also extend its original correspondence constructor and ordered source validation. Leave that extension separate under (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-chain-outstanding`).

Add opt-in `Heap::request_chained_pointwise_square`, `request_chained_pointwise_square_root` and `request_chained_pointwise_reciprocal`, with Square taking `PointwiseRequest<P>` and returning `ApplicationRequest<P>` in either native format; SquareRoot and Reciprocal take `PointwiseRequest<f64>` and return `ApplicationRequest<f64>`. They select the expected unary member and require application origin. Existing host entry points, including binary and prepared entry points, retain their accepted inputs, application-origin refusals, charged prefixes and controls unchanged. An internal admission mode selects the origin check; it introduces no persistent field, public completion constructor or separate planner. Use the same detached core, selected reader, traversal, publisher, cancellation and reconciliation. The actual pinned input's immutable origin selects composition validation on publication; a request cannot turn it off. The three opt-in entry points implement this unary-consumer slice through the common application core.

**Rule (Numerical error publication scope)** · `rule:nrz:matrix-loans-error-scope`

Application evidence records `NumericalErrorScope`, and `TileReader::numerical_error_scope` exposes it independently of source retirement. Host companions have `HostInput` scope. Mapped movement inherits the immediate input scope and copies its error bit-for-bit: a host copy reports `HostInput`, and a copy of an arithmetic result reports `LocalOperation`. The inherited scope is authenticated while the source is retained and survives its retirement (`[MATRIX-test:unit:loan-copy-square-error-scope]`). The scalar affine exception in (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-affine-numerics`) propagates the input error and reports `PropagatedInput` from host or already propagated inputs; copies preserve that scope, while a locally scoped input becomes `PropagatedLocal` without gaining host-chain coverage. Other arithmetic has `LocalOperation` scope: its Error bounds only the current operation's numerical discrepancy; inherited uncertainty participates in Total through the input total radius. Error is not a whole-chain numerical bound. The capture seal authenticates a copy's inherited scope against its retained source before evaluation. The member seal authenticates arithmetic scope before evaluation and publication. Literal small-integer Square continuations pin the local error to the independently encoded next float above the squared centre's upper ulp, after copying nonzero incoming error (`[MATRIX-test:unit:loan-mapped-square-gathered-both-formats]`), (`[MATRIX-test:unit:loan-mapped-square-product-and-outside-reader]`); copied errors retain their literal values (`[MATRIX-test:unit:loan-mapped-movement-original-companions]`). Complete whole-expression LC numerical-error propagation remains open; the scalar affine continuation is exercised by (`[MATRIX-test:unit:loan-projected-affine-current-lineage]`).

**Rule (Current origin and original correspondence for composition)** · `rule:nrz:matrix-loans-chain-origin`

First authenticate the supplied pin's continuation account and destination account, exact heap scope, live published group, pin-index pairing and numerical format through the current reader machinery in `loan/reader.rs`. Require `RepresentedInputs` in the member's admitted binary format, three declared ordinary roles, identity local incidence, equal C by k extents with k positive and the same original `CellAxis`. Admit only the five producer kinds named in (`dec:nrz:matrix-loans-chained-applications`); prepared evidence, retained law uses and stored endpoint attachments are unsupported. Match the group's application debit alternative and `Published` producer receipt to the bundle identity and origin. Validate the origin with `Origin::validate_current`: original checked operation and captured law pointer, member and revision, exclusion witnesses, source declaration and attachments, destination identity, completed-mask pointer and outcome extent must agree. For a binary producer this includes its side token, ordered original correspondence, both captured operands and empty published right-pin slot. Host import validation alone cannot establish this evidence, and equal readings, shapes or descriptor dimensions cannot replace it.

Composition additionally requires the recorded numerical lineage to be current. Starting at the authenticated immediate result, follow each captured `source_identity` to the exact published registry group; compare its declaration, value/companion symbols, qualified attachments, axis, extent, coverage and format with the capture, then run the same current-owner validation used by `reader.rs`. A unary ancestor continues this walk. A binary ancestor terminates it after validating Left and Right as the two distinct host origins required by (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-binary-correspondence`). A host terminates its branch. Check each group's origin against its host/unary/binary inventory and terminal receipt, including the absence of application evidence for a host. Reuse the immutable member evidence; do not rerun a kernel, rebuild an original operation, derive descriptors again or resolve a retired ordinary demand mask. This extra eligibility check belongs only to new composition and its publication. Ordinary current readers, ticket-to-pin acquisition and historical readers retain their source-independent behavior in (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-pointwise-states`), (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-binary-lifetime`). Thus a retained result can remain readable after its sources retire while being ineligible for a new chain.

Implement the walk with borrowed registry references and fixed local state: follow the unary spine iteratively and inspect the terminal binary pair in order. Every parent request identity must be strictly less than its child's, with matching heap scope and exact occupation generations; the nonwrapping allocator in `heap/identity.rs` supplies this order. The new destination receives its later identity from the existing admission block. Reject a self-edge, reversed edge or cycle as `WrongLoan` before allocation. No recursion, visited-set allocation, graph owner or uncharged depth traversal is needed. Foreign producing accounts are not by themselves foreign inputs: existing charged pin acquisition can establish a new continuation account. Authenticate that supplied pin account without treating historical producer accounts as interchangeable continuation ledgers.

Bind the next checked original at admission, using its existing `OperationDraft::map_elementwise` recipe, checked descriptor power, frozen input/output witnesses and full-shape structural exclusion under (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-pointwise-contract`), (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-pointwise-structure`). Match its input descriptor and literal shape to this exact published declaration, and its output descriptor to fresh independently declared result symbols with correct total/error targets. Capture the exact input identity, declaration, qualified attachments and demand in the existing `Evidence` fields alongside that original operation and law. At evaluation and publication compare the supplied pin to that capture and the one-cell law to the original law; at result access validate the immutable destination/completion seal. This binds an admitted numerical operation to the chosen published operand. It does not add an exact CAS equation relating independently declared result symbols to the transitive source expression; that separate correspondence remains open.

Refuse unpublished, cancelled, abandoned, foreign or stale occupations and mismatched declarations, generations, axes, shapes, coverage, targets and arithmetic revisions with the existing typed faults. A pending ticket still cannot supply a pin and retains `OwnershipPending`; chain admission adds no waiting state. A missing or retired lineage occupation is `StaleDependency`; inconsistent pin, origin, operand, inventory or completion pairings are `WrongLoan`; unsupported member/representation is `UnsupportedContract`. Preserve the more specific layout, coverage, target and structural refusals. Check the current storage epoch on the common preparation permit; do not confuse unrelated legacy relocation with replacement of a pinned managed occupation. Admission refuses before output/tracking allocation or numerical loads. Publication rechecks the complete current lineage and returns the same candidate in repair if it is stale. Repairing an allocation failure cannot refresh a retired source, swap an operand or legitimize old evidence.

**Rule (Chained demand and composed numerical meaning)** · `rule:nrz:matrix-loans-chain-demand`

For each step use a demand on its immediate input's exact layout. The sealed identity correspondence transports logical cell c and component j to the fresh output occupation; matching mask lengths alone is insufficient. Intersect demand with all three qualified input filled roles under (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-pointwise-demand`). Their shared immutable mask is already sealed to the producer's `Completed` outcomes; selected access also checks that outcome before any load. An upstream unfilled, unrequested, missing or refused cell is a hole: downstream demand there yields `MissingInput`, never a zero, a copied numerical refusal or permission to read initialized placeholders. The upstream typed refusal remains in its own outcome evidence. Outside downstream demand remains `NotRequested`. All selected components must succeed before the downstream cell completes; later cells are still evaluated after a local refusal. Empty demand, empty availability and all-refused publication are valid. No new per-cell outcome or mask backing is needed.

Each consumer applies its unchanged native law to the predecessor's published center x and total r, interpreted as the exact dyadic interval `[x-r,x+r]`. All three roles gate availability, but only center and total are loaded; error is already included in total. Preserve the existing outward input-box construction, domain checks, finite-range refusals, center rounding, total and nominal-error readings in (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-reciprocal-member`), (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-square-root-member`), (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-binary-numerics`). No clipping, recomputation from original host data, endpoint conversion or extra addition of upstream error is permitted. In particular a completed square-root enclosure can still reach zero and make the following reciprocal refuse, even when the exact root image is positive. A numerical refusal remains availability evidence; it is not an LC sign decision.

Containment composes on cells completed at every step: if the predecessor encloses the intended represented-input image and the next step encloses its function over that published interval, the new total encloses the composed image. The final nominal error still concerns only the final function at its immediate represented center; it is not the rounding error of the entire original expression. There is no fused law, tighter correlated-image claim, global error bound or requested chain tolerance without a separate derivation. Product retains original/per-cell allowances `21*L`/`21*k` and `check(2,max(21*k,3*L))`; each unary step separately retains `12*L`/`12*k` and `check(1,max(12*k,3*L))`. Both work and scratch ceilings apply at every step, including zero capacity. Consumption stays on each authenticated continuation's original cumulative account; invocation ceilings neither reset Work nor fund later steps.

**Rule (Immediate pins, lineage eligibility and chain release)** · `rule:nrz:matrix-loans-chain-lifetime`

Borrow the request pin throughout validation and fallible preparation, then transfer that exact already funded pin into the ordinary core only after all admission prerequisites succeed. Failure returns the identical request and pin. Ready, not-started, candidate, rejected and repair wrappers retain that core, immediate upstream bundle, cleanup receipt and final liveness witness. Evaluation reads only this pinned immutable bundle and can finish after heap destruction; it does not traverse the heap lineage or acquire ancestor pins. The source cannot retire, move or change its native mask while this use is live. The new origin walk is read-only and creates no retaining edges, so allocation inventory and destruction order remain those of (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-kernel-family-shared`).

Older ancestors are eligibility dependencies, not implicit numerical pins. Their registry roots ordinarily remain between sequential calls, but a caller intending to reclaim concurrently with a chain must retain any ancestors whose current availability it still needs through existing charged pins. Retiring an unpinned ancestor is permitted; a later chain admission or publication then refuses as stale. An immediate source's retirement waits for the downstream pin. A stale publication repair retains that pin and the computed candidate for cancellation; it cannot recover by resurrecting an occupation. This conservative current-lineage policy satisfies source-retirement refusal without silently retaining a whole graph or promising automatic last-use scheduling.

Successful publication installs the independent output and terminal producer before releasing the immediate source pin, one-cell owner and witness. Its sealed result retains symbolic source identity and its own outcomes, with no strong numerical parent. Repair releases neither pin nor evidence and never reevaluates. Cancellation from every detached state uses prepaid cleanup with no remaining evaluation Work; drop/unwind releases private allocations and the input edge before witness expiration. Existing reconciliation installs exactly one abandonment and releases conservative debits; successful publication/cancellation prevents a second terminal event. Extra source pins have independent lifetimes. Historical selected access to any retained chain result survives ancestor retirement and heap drop, but conveys no new composition authority. After all detached owners and result/ancestor pins are released and separately owned ordinary demand masks are retired, the existing bounded reconciliation and reclaim passes dispose of the entire chain, retaining only accounted vacant table capacity and undelivered local outcomes until drained. Forgotten capabilities keep their existing obligations; no destructor calls the heap or spends fresh Work.

